            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <windows>

WINDOWS



The windows in your house are older windows, though not the originals to the home, and they are of various styles. Most windows are older double or triple paned, vinyl framed windows, some with low e coatings, some without. A few windows at the back of the home are sliders, and I recommend those windows as your priority for an upgrade.



Upgrading specific windows in this house to ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 is something that could be considered as an upgrade for this home. If this is something the homeowner wishes to pursue I recommend full frame replacement windows to vinyl frames that are triple pane, double low-E glazed, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing all 14 windows in the home with ones that meet the criteria described above, you could potentially save $250 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.5 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however, there are rebates available from the Canada Greener Homes Grant. Please see their website for more details on program requirements.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</windows>
                <doors>

DOOR UPGRADE



The two exterioir doors in your home are older, but considered to be in reasonable condition. The savings on new doors will be minimal. Weather stripping was noted to be in decent shape at all locations, but the rear door was in need of some minor repairs. There are currenlty no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

VENTILATION



During the blower door test it was discovered that your house is quite air tight, at 2.56 air changes per hour.



If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed, rather than relying on natural air infiltration, or exhaust devices to provide a means of fresh air for your home.



Savings: Installing an Energy Star certified HRV that is a minimum 70% efficient and includes ECM blower fan motors is estimated to increase your energy costs by $130 per year and increase your annual energy usage by 2.9 GJ. By installing a HRV the ventilation of your house will be better managed and the comfort and indoor air quality for the occupants will be much better.



</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Propane Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 182 litre conventional electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save approximately $1040 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.





</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

FURNACE UPGRADE



Your current oil furnace is a mid efficient, direct vent furnace, which means it pulls its combustion air from outside, with no interaction from interior air and expels exhaust gas directly up the flue pipe. After our discussion, it's apparent that the oil furnace is used approximately 50% of the time to heat your home, and as such an upgrade should only be considered when the furnace is reaching the end of its useful life. At that time, consider making the switch to propane.



PROPANE FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled the following furnace upgrade option for you to consider in the future:



An ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website:



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program.



Savings:



By replacing your current oil furnace with a 96% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower - and considering current oil and propane prices - you could potentially save $80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Please be aware that the modelling is based on using your furnace to supply 100% of your heating needs, which as was mentioned to me during the evaluation, that is not how your home is primarily heated. See below for more information about supplementary heating. Also note, currently the cost savings do not justify making this switch in Yellowknife, however fuel prices are volatile, and therefore savings amounts change regularly.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



SUPPLEMENTARY HEATING - Wood Stove



You provided information during the evaluation that said you regularly use your wood stove to provide heat for your home. For modelling purposes I was able to model your home using your wood stove to provide 50% of your heating requirements. The modelling showed that by using your current wood stove 50% of the time you are saving approximately $670 per year in heating costs, depending on the price of heating oil. The savings shown here may seem lower than anticipated, but it is important to remember that the efficiency of the heating appliance plays a big role in cost savings. A less efficient appliance sends more heat up the flue as waste, as opposed to into your home. 



Another thing you can do to more evenly distribute the heat from your wood stove (which is intended as a space heater) is to use the furnace fan to circulate the heat. In an older furnace like yours, this will add to your electricity bill in a small way, but if you were to upgrade the fan to an ECM fan, or upgrade to a new furnace including an ECM fan, the heat distribution cost would be minimal.



WOOD STOVE UPGRADE



It was also discussed that you could consider upgrading the wood stove to a newer stove in the near future. The savings for this upgrade would be minimal given the efficiency of the new vs old wood stove would not change significantly. The savings don't justify the upgrade in this case, however, if your current wood stove is reaching the end of it's useful life then it's something to consider.





</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

SOLAR PV



SOLAR PV GENERATION:  Hydro Community



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



Savings: By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the south facing roof of your house you could potentially save $1000 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11 GJ. 



If you choose the upgrade path of installing solar panels, you should stick with an electric hot water tank. Please speak with a solar installer to discuss appropriate sizing of the system for your home.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife, however there are incentives available from the Canada Greener Homes Program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,000 per KW is available for installing a photovoltaic solar panel. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards.







</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

WATER CONSERVATION



Your house has an older 13 liter toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!



Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings. 



There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



 In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

 If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

 Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

 All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

 It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

 All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 11.4 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your wood stove vents up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting. Newer wood stoves get their combustion air directly from outside.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

       

        - two bathroom fans

        - a range hood

        - a clothes dryer

      

You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs  Yellowknife



The following December, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.25 per liter 

Propane - $1.00 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.99 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered





